# Content

`src/content/site.ts` is the **single source of truth for all copy**. Components render
it and contain no prose. Change the bio, a job entry, or a case study here.

## Rules that are not stylistic preferences

These came out of a design process; they encode real constraints (ADR-0005).

- **Never invent a metric.** Every number on the site traces to the CV: 7s→2s at
  WP Engine, 5min→1.5min at Fexco. A new figure needs confirming with Nazmul first.
- **Clients are named; their systems are genericised.** Naming Cisco, Fidelity, HMH or
  Optum is fine — all are public on the CV and LinkedIn. Describing their internal
  architecture, proprietary identifiers, or unpublished metrics is not.
- **The text is written in the first person on Nazmul's behalf.** It makes claims about
  his work. Do not add a claim he has not made.
- **The term "vibe coding" is deliberately absent.** Show the practice, not the label.

## Voice

Direct, specific, first person. No emoji, no "passionate developer", no skill-percentage
bars, no tech-logo walls. Prefer a concrete constraint over an adjective.

## Case study shape

Each is `Context → Constraints → What I did → Trade-offs → Outcome` (headings vary
slightly). **The trade-offs section is the point** — it is what separates a case study
from a job description. Name the alternative that was rejected and why.

## Structure

- `site` — identity, contact, meta description
- `heroLine` — one sentence under the name. Specific beats comprehensive
- `nowText` — current work
- `caseStudies` — the four detailed engagements; `slug` maps to the route
- `experience` — full dated history, including roles without a case study
- `background` — education, certifications, volunteering, personal note
